So the compiler uses `Code->getExpr().getInstrs()`. But in AOT mode, the loader skips parsing the function body. So `CodeSeg.getExpr()` would be empty (no instructions).

Wait, but then `FC.compile(*Code, ...)` would compile an empty instruction list. Let me look at `compile(CodeSegment, Type)` again:

```cpp
void compile(const AST::CodeSegment &Code,
             std::pair<std::vector<ValType>, std::vector<ValType>> Type) noexcept {
    auto RetBB = LLVM::BasicBlock::create(LLContext, F.Fn, "ret");
    Type.first.clear();
    enterBlock(RetBB, {}, {}, {}, std::move(Type));
    compile(Code.getExpr().getInstrs());
    assuming(ControlStack.empty());
    compileReturn();
    ...
}
```

If `Code.getExpr().getInstrs()` is empty, then `compile(Instrs)` does nothing, and `compileReturn()` is called. The function has return type f64 (non-void), so `stackPop()` is called on the empty stack → crash!

But wait, in my test module `(func (result i32) unreachable)`, the body was `[unreachable, end]`. In AOT mode, the loader skips the body, so `Code.getExpr().getInstrs()` is empty. Then `compileReturn()` is called with empty stack → should crash too!

But my test didn't crash. Why?

Hmm, let me reconsider. Maybe the loader doesn't skip the body in my case. Let me re-read the condition:

```cpp
if (!Conf.getRuntimeConfigure().isForceInterpreter() &&
    WASMType != InputType::WASM) {
    FMgr.seek(ExprSizeBound);
}
```

`WASMType` - what is this? Let me check. It might be that `WASMType` is `InputType::WASM` by default, in which case the body IS parsed. Let me check.

Actually, wait. Let me reconsider. The `WASMType` might be set based on the file extension or some other factor. In the fuzztool, the input is raw bytes, so `WASMType` might default to `InputType::WASM`, meaning the body IS parsed.

Let me check what `WASMType` is.

